WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.204
File handling usually begins as ordinary
utility code.

2
00:00:03.284 --> 00:00:06.506
A Java application adds a report download,
writes a temporary PDF,

3
00:00:06.586 --> 00:00:09.808
reads a template, moves an uploaded file,
or serves a static asset.

4
00:00:09.888 --> 00:00:13.110
Later, the same application may add
exports, archive processing,

5
00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:16.700
object storage, generated invoices, cached
data, background workers,

6
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:17.931
and private downloads.

7
00:00:18.011 --> 00:00:20.830
At that point, file handling is no longer
just helper code.

8
00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.247
It is a security boundary that decides
what data can be read, written,

9
00:00:24.327 --> 00:00:26.628
transformed, retained, and returned to
users.

10
00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:30.448
Unsafe file handling can expose source
code, secrets, configuration, logs,

11
00:00:30.528 --> 00:00:34.038
customer data, generated reports, backups,
or another tenant's files.

12
00:00:34.118 --> 00:00:36.880
The risk can come from path traversal,
unsafe filenames,

13
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.297
excessive permissions, symbolic link
surprises, race conditions,

14
00:00:40.377 --> 00:00:43.541
resource exhaustion, unsafe temporary
files, public exposure,

15
00:00:43.621 --> 00:00:46.210
archive extraction mistakes, and metadata
leakage.

16
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.994
Many of these issues do not look dramatic
in code review.

17
00:00:49.074 --> 00:00:52.181
They look like a string concatenation, a
convenient directory,

18
00:00:52.261 --> 00:00:55.656
a missing authorization check, or a read
that assumes the path is safe.

19
00:00:55.736 --> 00:00:58.900
Java applications use many file-related
APIs and environments:

20
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.202
java.nio.file, java.io, Servlet and Spring
download endpoints,

21
00:01:02.282 --> 00:01:05.965
Jakarta EE services, JAX-RS resources,
batch jobs, CLI tools, containers,

22
00:01:06.045 --> 00:01:08.289
mounted volumes, and object storage
clients.

23
00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:11.821
Each tool can support a safe design, but
no single API call is the design.

24
00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:15.756
A call to normalize, a framework resource
helper, a filename cleanup function,

25
00:01:15.836 --> 00:01:17.562
or a storage bucket policy can help,

26
00:01:17.642 --> 00:01:21.554
but it cannot decide whether the user is
authorized or whether the operation fits

27
00:01:21.634 --> 00:01:22.785
the business workflow.

28
00:01:22.865 --> 00:01:26.432
The goal is to make file access
intentional, bounded, least-privileged,

29
00:01:26.512 --> 00:01:27.835
observable, and testable.

30
00:01:27.915 --> 00:01:30.677
A reviewer should be able to answer where
files come from,

31
00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:33.749
where they may be stored, who may read
them, who may write them,

32
00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:37.339
what limits apply, how temporary files are
cleaned up, what gets logged,

33
00:01:37.419 --> 00:01:38.857
and how failures are handled.

34
00:01:38.937 --> 00:01:42.102
This course focuses on defensive design
and engineering review,

35
00:01:42.182 --> 00:01:43.620
not exploit demonstrations.
